% 1) WE say that action distinctions are lost in normal FB since it focuses on the larger pat of shared occupancy under a linear fit 
% 2) So, we need a way to preserve these distinctions while still learning a successor measure 
% 3) Density FB instead learns a probability distribution 
% 4) This distribution enables the zero shot properties, but can preserve action distinctions

Section~\ref{sec} showed that joint linear fitting can erase differences between actions' future occupancies.
\name{} addresses this limitation by placing the low-rank constraint on the scores used to generate occupancies and converting them into a probability distribution with a softmax.
Because this conversion is nonlinear, low-rank scores can generate occupancies that are not themselves low-rank.
The same number of features can therefore represent distinct futures for different actions that a direct linear fit may merge.
\ben{I don't really follow the arguments here. Standard FB is learning a density (ratio), so the ``density'' part of our method isn't unique. And the representations learned by FB are nonlinear functions of the action, so I'm not sure about the linearity assumption. I agree that we're using a CE loss instead of a LSIF loss.}
We fit these distributions to Bellman targets using cross-entropy (\cref{sec}), training the scores to capture how each action changes the likelihood of future states.
We then compute action values by averaging future rewards under the predicted distributions (\cref{sec}).

\subsection{Learning a normalized successor distribution}
\label{sec:method-learning}

For a future state $s^+$, we define a bilinear score and normalize its exponential to obtain a successor density relative to $\rho$:
\begin{equation}
        f_\theta(s,a,z,s^+) :=\frac{F_\theta(s,a,z)^\top B_\theta(s^+)}{\tau}, \qquad  h_\theta(s,a,z,s^+) :=\frac{\exp f_\theta(s,a,z,s^+)} {\E_{\tilde s^+\sim\rho}[\exp f_\theta(s,a,z,\tilde s^+)]},
    \label{eq:density-model}
\end{equation}
where $\tau>0$ is a temperature and $\tilde s^+$ is a reference state drawn from $\rho$.
We define the predicted SM by $\widehat M_\theta(s,a,z,ds^+):=h_\theta(s,a,z,s^+)\rho(ds^+)$.
The normalization makes this a probability measure since $h_\theta$ is nonnegative and integrates to one under $\rho$.
The bilinear scores determine the relative density of future states; exponentiation and normalization convert these scores into the successor distribution.
The following proposition shows that joint fitting of this model can preserve an action distinction lost by the linear fit at the same feature dimension.

