\begin{proposition}[Preservation of a bottleneck distinction]
\label{prop:bottleneck-preservation}
For the actions $a_+,a_-$ at a control bottleneck (\cref{def:control-bottleneck}), there exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that any Density-FB fit satisfying
\[
    D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left( M^\pi(s,a,\cdot) \,\middle\|\, \widehat M_\theta(s,a,z,\cdot) \right)<\varepsilon, \qquad a\in\{a_+,a_-\},
\]
preserves their action ordering:
\[
    \widehat Q_r^\pi(s,a_+)>\widehat Q_r^\pi(s,a_-).
\]
\end{proposition}



On this construction, joint softmax fitting preserves the action ordering that joint linear fitting loses at the same feature dimension. 
The proof is given in \cref{app:bottleneck-proof}.
This comparison uses exact SMs as fitting targets.
To learn from offline transitions, we instead construct bootstrapped distributional targets using the SM Bellman equation.

We learn a family of policies $\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z)$, with actor parameters $\omega$.
Task vectors are drawn from a distribution $p_Z$ that mixes random vectors with indices inferred from sampled goal-reaching rewards using the encoder in \cref{app:task-index}.
For a minibatch $\{(s_i,a_i,s_i',\mathrm{terminal}_i)\}_{i=1}^n$, we use its next states as candidate future states, writing $s_j^+:=s_j'$.
Normalizing the scores over these candidates gives a length $n$ vector of probabilities, $p(s, a, z) \in \mathcal{P}^n$ indicating the likelihood of each state $s_i^+$ being a future state, with each coordinate $j$ given by
\begin{equation}
    p_j(s,a,z) :=\frac{\exp f_\theta(s,a,z,s_j^+)} {\sum_{\ell=1}^n\exp f_\theta(s,a,z,s_\ell^+)}.
    \label{eq:density-candidates}
\end{equation}
% the predicted probability assigned to candidate $j$.


The Bellman equation in \eqref{eq:sm-bellman} combines the observed next state with the distribution of subsequent states.
For a non-terminal transition, the target assigns weight $1-\gamma$ to the observed next-state candidate and weight $\gamma$ to the target network's successor distribution from that state.
We fit these target probabilities by cross-entropy:
\begin{align}
    % p_{ij} &:=p_j(s_i,a_i,z_i), \qquad \bar p_{ij}' :=\bar p_j(s_i',a_i',z_i), \qquad a_i'\sim\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s_i',z_i), \nonumber\\
    &t_{ij} :=\sg\!\left[(1-d_i)\ind{j=i}+d_i\bar p_j(s_i',a_i',z_i)\right], \quad a_i'\sim\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s_i',z_i), \; d_i:=\gamma(1-\mathrm{terminal}_i), \nonumber\\
    &\mathcal L_{\mathrm{DFB}} :=-\frac1n\sum_{i,j}t_{ij}\log p_j(s_i,a_i,z_i),
    \label{eq:density-loss}
\end{align}
\ben{Is this exactly the same as TD InfoNCE? If so, it might be simpler to introduce the TD InfoNCE loss in the prelims as a method for policy evaluation. Then, in this section, we can say that we're applying it to evaluate the density of skill conditioned policies.}
where $\bar p_j$ uses target networks $(\bar F,\bar B)$ over the same candidates, and $\sg$ stops gradients through the target.
For terminal transitions, the target puts all mass on $s_i'$, treating that state as absorbing.
This update uses the next-state and bootstrapped-future structure of TD InfoNCE~\citep{zheng-iclr24a}, with the policy conditioned on a task vector $z$.
\Cref{sec:exp-didactic} evaluates action-distinction preservation under TD training.

