\subsection{Using the learned distribution for control}
\label{sec:method-control}

FB's linear value readout relies on its bilinear output representing a successor density.
In \name{}, that output represents a score, so we evaluate actions by integrating rewards under the normalized distribution.
For a state reward $r$ and candidate future states, \eqref{eq:sm-value} gives the estimate
\begin{equation}
    \widehat Q_{\theta,r}(s,a,z) :=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\sum_{j=1}^n p_j(s,a,z)r(s_j^+).
    \label{eq:density-value}
\end{equation}
Note that $p(s, a, z)$ is a categorical distribution over $n$ possible future states, so it sums to one.
\ben{Readers might wonder: how does this compare to just naively using $Q(s, a, z) = F(s, a, z)^Tz$, as done by FB?}

\ben{The following paragraph is kinda hard to parse. How is this different from what we've already introduced around Eq 9?}
Applying the same reward averaging to the successor target gives $Y_{i,r}:=(1-\gamma)^{-1}\sum_jt_{ij}r(s_j^+)$.
Writing $\bar Q_{i,r}'=(1-\gamma)^{-1}\sum_j\bar p_{ij}'r(s_j^+)$ for the target-network value, \eqref{eq:density-loss} yields $Y_{i,r}=\frac{1-d_i}{1-\gamma}r(s_i')+d_i\bar Q_{i,r}'$.
For non-terminal transitions, this recovers the usual sampled Bellman target $Y_{i,r}=r(s_i')+\gamma\bar Q_{i,r}'$.
For terminal transitions, it gives $r(s_i')/(1-\gamma)$, consistent with the absorbing-state convention.

During pretraining, we use the latent reward induced by the dot product $B(s^+)$ and $z$, $r_z(s^+):=B_\theta(s^+)^\top z/\sqrt d$.
\ben{This reward seems a bit like magic. Can you explain a bit where it comes from?}
For actor updates, we evaluate this reward using the centered features $B_\xi(s^+)-\hat\mu$, where $B_\xi$ is a slowly updated copy of the backward representation and $\hat\mu$ is the running feature mean (\cref{app:task-index}).
Policy improvement maximizes the resulting value estimates:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal J_{\mathrm{value}}(\omega) :=\E_{\substack{ s\sim\mathcal D,\;z\sim p_Z\\ a\sim\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z) }}\!\left[\widehat Q_{\theta,r_z}(s,a,z)\right].
    \label{eq:density-policy-objective}
\end{equation}
For offline training, we combine this objective with behavior regularization.
The full actor objectives and value scaling are given in \cref{app:actor}.
At evaluation, we infer $z_r$ from reward-labeled offline states using the encoder in \cref{app:task-index} and execute the frozen policy $\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z_r)$.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
    \caption{\name{} training and zero-shot control}
    \label{alg:density-fb}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \Require Offline dataset $\mathcal D$, successor networks $(F_\theta,B_\theta)$, target networks $(\bar F,\bar B)$, actor $\pi_\omega$
        \For{each training step}
            \State Sample transitions and task vectors from $\mathcal D$ and $p_Z$
            \State Use the sampled next states as candidate future states
            \State Normalize online and target scores over the candidates (\cref{eq:density-candidates})
            \State Form the Bellman target distribution and update $(F_\theta,B_\theta)$ by cross-entropy (\cref{eq:density-loss})
            \State Update $\pi_\omega$ using the reward-averaged values and behavior regularization, with $(F_\theta,B_\theta)$ fixed (\cref{eq:density-policy-objective})
            \State Update target networks, reward features $B_\xi$, and running feature mean $\hat\mu$
        \EndFor
        \Statex
        \Statex \textbf{Zero-shot control for downstream reward $r$}
        \State Infer $z_r$ from reward-labeled offline states
        \State Execute the frozen policy $\pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s,z_r)$
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}